f one another.\n2. Homogeneity of Variance: The variance among the experimental units should be constant across treatments.\n3. Normality: The response variable should be approximately normally distributed for each treatment group.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nExperimental units are applicable in any experimental design where treatments are applied to subjects or items. The selection of experimental units should consider the nature of the treatment, the scale of measurement, and the desired precision of the results. Researchers must ensure that the experimental units are representative of the population being studied.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of data collected from experimental units typically involves statistical methods such as ANOVA, regression analysis, or mixed models, depending on the design and the nature of the data. The analysis aims to determine whether there are significant differences in the response variable across the treatments applied to the experimental units.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Clear definition of treatment application allows for precise measurement of treatment effects.\n- Facilitates the replication of experiments, enhancing the reliability of results.\n- Enables the use of statistical methods to analyze and interpret data effectively.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Misidentification of experimental units can lead to biased results and incorrect conclusions.\n- The need for sufficient experimental units to achieve statistical power can limit the feasibility of some experiments.\n- Variability among experimental units can complicate the analysis and interpretation of results.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nExperimental units are foundational to various experimental designs, including:\n- Completely Randomized Design (CRD)\n- Randomized Block Design (RBD)\n- Factorial Design\n- Split-Plot Design\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implement